#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Wave 35: Little Hands, Just Three, a Sixth Time — the sixth 3-only wave.

Wave 34's honest assessment named this wave the same way waves
29-33 named theirs: the age-3 gap (118 against 160 at age 4, gap
42) "closes only by more waves of this kind, at honest quality,
or not at all." Five consecutive 3-only waves have narrowed it
102 -> 90 -> 78 -> 66 -> 54 -> 42. This is the sixth: twelve
more records banded 3-3, built to the rules waves 30-34 proved —
short (<= 20 minutes), adult-led, big pieces, one rule per game,
the adult as a player — because the band is honest for the same
reason: these are three-year-old moments (a smile passed hand to
hand round a circle without ever being dropped, a puddle that
holds the whole sky until somebody's shadow crosses it, one
rainstick tipped so slowly the rain inside it takes a full
minute to fall), and age 4, holding 160 records, does not miss
them.

Measured before this wave (primary category = categories[0],
records covering age 3): holidays and team-community were the
floor at 9 each; seasons / stem / icebreakers / outdoor /
music-rhythm 10 each; crafts 11, pe-movement 11, rainy-day 14,
quiet-focus 14. Rainy-day (35) is still joint-thinnest primary
category on the whole shelf with music-rhythm (35). So the pack
repeats the proven shape exactly: one record for every one of
the 11 categories — the floor rises 9 -> 10 everywhere — plus a
second rainy-day record. Result, asserted in the wave log:
age 3 118 -> 130, age 4 unchanged at 160, the headline gap
42 -> 30, narrowed for the sixth wave running. Tag deltas:
MLK Day 5 -> 6 (one of the last two holiday tags on the floor
of 5 rises), summer 30 -> 31 (the thinnest season rises).

Near-duplicate screen: token Jaccard >= 0.55 against every
existing name+tagline is asserted below and fails the run.
Closest deliberate neighbours, kept on purpose:
- big-dream-echo-circle vs dream-speech-choral-reading (older
  kids reading a famous speech aloud in parts): no text is read
  here and no speech is studied — the adult says one short
  dream sentence of the group's own, the circle echoes it
  whole, and each child may add one dream word of their own.
  Studying a dream vs saying one at three-year-old size.
- big-warm-stone-hold-tell vs sun-patch-shade-patch (3-only,
  wave 29: finding sunny and shady spots with your feet): the
  patches there are walked between and compared by standing;
  the stones here are HELD, one warm and one cool in two hands,
  and the comparison is reported from the palms, seated.
- big-bus-stop-ride-along vs cushion-train-all-aboard (a train
  of cushions the group boards and rides in a line): the train
  there travels by its cars shuffling; the bus here never moves
  at all — the journey is the STOPS, called by the driver, and
  getting on and off with a big token is the whole curriculum.
- big-leg-tunnel-ball-roll vs blanket-ball-bounce-together (a
  ball kept bouncing on a held blanket): nothing is bounced and
  nobody holds anything — the ball ROLLS, through a tunnel made
  of the group's own standing legs, and the tunnel, not a
  blanket, is the team's work.
- big-heavy-light-hand-balance vs pan-balance-heavy-light (older
  kids, a beam balance and predictions): no beam, no scale, no
  predictions — the instrument is the child's own two hands,
  one object in each, and the report is which hand sinks.
- big-one-statue-many-bodies vs human-machine (older kids, a
  machine of moving linked parts): the machine there MOVES and
  makes its product; the statue here is FROZEN as one shape the
  whole group holds together, and holding still, together, is
  the entire achievement.
- big-pass-the-smile-circle vs pass-the-face (older kids passing
  exaggerated expressions round a circle for laughs): faces
  there are jokes, copied bigger each time; the smile here is
  passed at its own true size, kindly, one neighbour at a time,
  and it is never laughed at — being smiled at is the payload.
- big-puddle-sky-mirror-look vs puddle-stomp-parade (the puddle
  as a thing to stomp through, gleefully): the stomp there IS
  the activity; here the puddle is looked INTO, never stepped
  in, because it is holding a picture of the sky — and the one
  permitted toe-touch at the very end is the picture closing.
- big-rainstick-tip-and-listen vs rainstick-rhythm-circle
  (older kids, rainsticks keeping a beat together): no beat is
  kept here — one stick, tipped once per holder, as slowly as
  hands can tip it, and the group listens to the rain fall
  inside it. Rhythm vs listening; many sticks vs one.
- big-bubble-wrap-print-paint vs bubble-wrap-stomp-path (bubble
  wrap as a floor path to POP underfoot): the wrap there is
  destroyed by feet, on purpose, for sound; the wrap here is a
  printing tool that must NOT pop — pressed flat into paint and
  lifted straight, it stamps its circles and survives to stamp
  again. Popping is this record's one failure mode.
- big-log-roll-down-the-mat vs grass-hill-roll-down (3-only
  starter, rolling down a real hill outdoors): the hill there
  is grass, gravity, and joy; the mat here is flat, indoors,
  and the skill is rolling STRAIGHT — arms in, chin tucked, the
  whole long body turning like a log along one taped line.
- big-wooden-bead-stringing vs big-peg-clip-card-row (wave 33,
  spring pegs pinched onto cards): the peg there opens against
  a spring and grips; the bead here is threaded — found, aimed,
  pushed along a stiff lace — and the finished string is worn,
  counted, and unthreaded back into the bowl by its own maker.

Safety notes are practical only — no medical claims anywhere.

Run once: dedupes by id, writes data/activities.json + collections.json.
Run scripts/build.py afterwards.
"""
